---
import PresentationLayout from '../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro';
---

<PresentationLayout title="Bridging Museum Collections and Living Buddhist Communities">

	<!-- SLIDE 1: Where Do I Speak From -->
	<section class="slide relative min-h-screen flex items-center overflow-hidden bg-white">
		<!-- Background image -->
		<div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
			<img
				src="/pictures/bird eye view of Swayambhu.png"
				alt="Bird's eye view of Swayambhu"
				class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-20"
			/>
		</div>

		<div class="relative z-10 max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 w-full">
			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-16 items-center">
				<!-- Left: Content -->
				<div class="relative">
					<div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-[#f5b611] bg-opacity-20 border-2 border-[#f5b611] mb-6">
						<span class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase">
							My Background
						</span>
					</div>
					<h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl lg:text-7xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
						Where Do I Speak From?
					</h1>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed mb-4 opacity-90">
						I come to this work through <strong>art and cultural preservation</strong>, not politics. My father, Tarthang Tulku Rinpoche, and I have dedicated our lives to Buddhist cultural preservation.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed mb-4 opacity-90">
						In 2008, I directed the renovation of <strong>Swayambhu Stupa</strong> in Nepal—a UNESCO World Heritage site and one of the world's most ancient Buddhist monuments. This was the <strong>fifteenth renovation in 1,500 years</strong>, and I was the first woman to lead such a project.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed opacity-90">
						Over two years, we worked with <strong>70+ traditional artisans</strong>, renovated <strong>30,000+ individual pieces</strong>, and created meticulous documentation: <strong>60,000 images and 2,500 pages of reports</strong>.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Right: Image -->
				<div class="relative">
					<div class="rounded-[12px] overflow-hidden border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
						<img
							src="/pictures/Swayambhu renovation.png"
							alt="Swayambhu Stupa renovation"
							class="w-full h-full object-cover"
						/>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 1 continued: What I Learned -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-[#f9f2e6]">
		<div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					The Lesson
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					What I Learned About Museums
				</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-12 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7] mb-8">
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed mb-6">
					We were able to complete the renovation based on <strong>archival photos that we found in Nepal and at museums</strong>. We looked at their documentation of the art and architectural details, and based on that evidence, we recreated elements that had been lost or damaged.
				</p>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed font-medium">
					The museum resources gave us tangible guidance that the community could engage with meaningfully.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
				<p class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed">
					This experience showed me the incredible value that museum collections can offer to living communities when there's a bridge connecting them.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 2: The Three Sacreds -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-white">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					Understanding the Framework
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					The Three Sacreds
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto">
					My father, Tarthang Tulku Rinpoche, has always emphasized these three elements. They aren't separate—they're deeply interconnected.
				</p>
			</div>

			<!-- Three cards -->
			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8 mb-12">
				<!-- Sacred Object -->
				<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7]">
					<div class="w-20 h-20 mx-auto mb-6">
						<img src="/icons/noun-mandala-6259529.svg" alt="" class="w-full h-full" style="filter: invert(87%) sepia(13%) saturate(1026%) hue-rotate(106deg) brightness(92%) contrast(92%);" />
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-4 text-center">Sacred Object</h3>
					<p class="text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed text-center">
						A thangka painting, a statue, a ritual implement—vehicles for study, contemplation, and realization
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Sacred Person -->
				<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
					<div class="w-20 h-20 mx-auto mb-6">
						<img src="/icons/noun-lotus-6259531.svg" alt="" class="w-full h-full" style="filter: invert(58%) sepia(87%) saturate(457%) hue-rotate(358deg) brightness(102%) contrast(93%);" />
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-4 text-center">Sacred Person</h3>
					<p class="text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed text-center">
						The practitioner or community who engages with the object through study and practice
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Sacred Place -->
				<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#d45c26]">
					<div class="w-20 h-20 mx-auto mb-6">
						<img src="/icons/noun-stupa-7981677.svg" alt="" class="w-full h-full" style="filter: invert(34%) sepia(61%) saturate(1095%) hue-rotate(338deg) brightness(95%) contrast(88%);" />
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-4 text-center">Sacred Place</h3>
					<p class="text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed text-center">
						The temple, the altar, the stupa, the monastery—where objects and persons come together
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Bottom section: Visual Transmission -->
			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-3xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">
						The Dharma Has Always Been Transmitted Visually
					</h3>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						The Tibetan Buddhist tradition has an unusually large output of sacred art. Images are vehicles for study, for contemplation, for realization.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						When a young monk learns about Avalokiteshvara, he's studying the iconography: the thousand arms, the eleven heads, the specific hand gestures.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg font-[Cabin] font-semibold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed">
						The image teaches in ways that words cannot.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="relative">
					<div class="rounded-[12px] overflow-hidden border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
						<img
							src="/pictures/Yab Yum.jpg"
							alt="Yab-Yum deity"
							class="w-full h-full object-cover aspect-[4/3]"
						/>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 2 continued: The Restitution Debate -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-[#f9f2e6]">
		<div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					The Restitution Debate
				</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-12 border-4 border-[#d45c26] mb-8">
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed mb-6">
					I recognize there's an enormous debate about restitution and responsibility. There are genuine tensions that need to be worked out through international dialogue, legal frameworks, and community consultation.
				</p>
				<p class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed mb-6">
					But let me be clear about something:
				</p>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed">
					Even if these objects were returned to their communities, that alone wouldn't be enough. It wouldn't be meaningful unless there was a <strong>deep educational component</strong> built into it. Otherwise, it's just another location.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
				<p class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed">
					Today I want to talk about a practical pathway to make museum collections serve living education and living practice in Buddhist monastic communities.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 3: The Overview Effect -->
	<section class="slide relative min-h-screen flex items-center overflow-hidden bg-black">
		<div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
			<img
				src="/pictures/Earth from Space.jpg"
				alt="Earth from space"
				class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-60"
			/>
		</div>

		<div class="relative z-10 max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 w-full text-center">
			<div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-[#f5b611] bg-opacity-30 border-2 border-[#f5b611] mb-8">
				<span class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-white uppercase">March 2025</span>
			</div>

			<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-white mb-8 leading-tight" style="text-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
				The Overview Effect
			</h2>

			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto bg-gray-800 bg-opacity-80 rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-12 mb-8">
				<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl text-white leading-relaxed mb-6">
					Earlier this year, our Institute participated in <strong>LunARC</strong>—a project that carried a nano-chip containing thousands of pieces of art to the moon, where they'll be preserved for millions of years.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg text-white opacity-90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
					There's a term astronauts use called the <strong>"overview effect."</strong> When you see the Earth from space, you can't see the borders between countries. You just see this beautiful blue planet, and you understand that we share one home, one interconnected ecosystem, and that life is precious beyond words.
				</p>
				<p class="text-xl text-white font-[Cabin] font-bold leading-relaxed">
					If we can think about preserving art for millions of years on the moon—if we can think about these objects as belonging to humanity—that might change the conversation about conservation.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 4: The Gap & Why SINI -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-white">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					The Challenge
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					The Gap & Why SINI
				</h2>
			</div>

			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 mb-12">
				<!-- The Gap -->
				<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#d45c26]">
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">The Gap</h3>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						Traditional Tibetan Buddhist monastic education is extraordinarily rigorous—a geshe studies for <strong>15 to 25 years</strong>.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						But <strong>art history, material culture studies, and conservation science</strong> are generally not included.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
						Meanwhile, museums hold extraordinary resources that these communities could benefit from, but these two worlds rarely connect.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Why SINI -->
				<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7]">
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">Why SINI</h3>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						SINI was founded by my father in <strong>Sarnath, India</strong>, one of the most sacred sites in Buddhism, where the Buddha gave his first teaching.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						For decades, he's been <strong>reproducing sacred art, including from museum collections</strong>, and distributing them to monasteries and practitioners.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
						We've had students from all four schools of Tibetan Buddhism. We could serve as a <strong>platform, a stepping stone</strong> between museums and hundreds of small monasteries.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Image -->
			<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
				<div class="rounded-[12px] overflow-hidden border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
					<img
						src="/pictures/monks in SINI campus.jpg"
						alt="Monks at SINI campus"
						class="w-full h-full object-cover"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 5: Digital Access -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-[#f9f2e6]">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					Proposal 1
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					Digital Access
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto">
					Making museum collections accessible and usable for monastic education
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-10 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7] mb-8">
				<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">Training & Access</h3>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					A series of <strong>online trainings for Buddhist communities</strong> on how to access existing digital collections. Once they know what's available, the next step is how to use these materials for their study.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg font-[Cabin] font-semibold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed">
					Something being available, something being accessible, and something being used are three different things.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-10 border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
				<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">User Experience: The Sacred Art Bank</h3>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					When a geshe or monk thinks about these objects, they're not thinking "Pitt Rivers Museum" or "the Louvre." Those names mean nothing to them.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					What's meaningful are the <strong>sacred sites</strong>—Sarnath, Bodh Gaya, Kushinagar, Lumbini—or they're thinking about <strong>lineage</strong>.
				</p>
				<p class="text-xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] leading-relaxed mb-4">
					Imagine a collective Sacred Art Bank that organized objects by sacred site and lineage.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
					All the Sarnath objects from museums around the world—London, Paris, Frankfurt, New York—searchable together as "Sarnath Images." All the Gelug lineage objects, or Nyingma lineage objects, grouped together.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 6: Digital Repair -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-white">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					Proposal 2
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					Digital Repair
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto">
					Creating respectful, complete images for educational purposes
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center mb-8">
				<div>
					<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#d45c26]">
						<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">The Student Perspective</h3>
						<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
							I've been having conversations with students at SINI to understand how they would actually use museum images.
						</p>
						<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
							If there's an important statue in a museum collection, but it's broken or damaged, they would prefer it to be presented <strong>respectfully—not broken</strong>.
						</p>
						<p class="text-lg font-[Cabin] font-semibold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed">
							They would be more likely to use an image that was more respectful and complete.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div>
					<div class="bg-[#f9f2e6] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7]">
						<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">The Solution</h3>
						<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
							In the digital world, we could <strong>repair and restore images specifically for educational purposes</strong>.
						</p>
						<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
							The museums preserve the art as it is, unchanged. But for educational use, we could digitally fill in the missing parts.
						</p>
						<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
							Students also want images <strong>lightened up, enhanced for clarity</strong>, so the details they need to study are clear and accessible.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto text-center bg-gradient-to-r from-[#9ed7c7] to-[#f5b611] rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#d45c26]">
				<p class="text-xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-white leading-relaxed" style="text-shadow: 0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);">
					This kind of digital enhancement doesn't compromise the original; it creates teaching tools that serve living practice.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 7: The Sacred Art Exchange -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-[#f9f2e6]">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					Proposal 3
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					The Sacred Art Exchange
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xl text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto">
					Curriculum work through exchanges—museums working with our communities in meaningful educational partnerships
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-12">
				<!-- Fellows to Museums -->
				<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#9ed7c7]">
					<div class="text-4xl mb-6 text-center">→</div>
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 text-center">SINI → Museums</h3>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						Graduates of our program could come and be hosted at museums to see firsthand the objects.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
						Fellows could study collections, learn conservation techniques, contribute their knowledge, and create educational resources.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Curators to SINI -->
				<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 border-4 border-[#f5b611]">
					<div class="text-4xl mb-6 text-center">←</div>
					<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 text-center">Museums → SINI</h3>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
						Curators could come to SINI to share their knowledge and learn about the three sacreds.
					</p>
					<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed">
						They could see how these materials are used in living practice and education.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Example: Kangyur Karchak -->
			<div class="bg-white rounded-[12px] p-8 md:p-10 border-4 border-[#d45c26]">
				<h3 class="text-2xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6">Example: Visual Education at SINI</h3>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					When we were doing text preservation work with the Kangyur Karchak, we did an incredible exercise. Students and translators were taking notes about particular Buddha teachings—a sutra might describe deer, squirrels, a purple lotus, mountains, 500 monks.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg text-[#453c36] opacity-80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
					If that sutra didn't have a widely known illustration, <strong>we created images</strong>. Our artist Kaveri Singh hand-painted these stories from the Buddha's life.
				</p>
				<p class="text-lg font-[Cabin] font-semibold text-[#d45c26] leading-relaxed">
					We took the story that was written and transformed it into an image. It became such a powerful teaching tool: most people are visual learners.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- SLIDE 8: What Success Looks Like -->
	<section class="slide py-20 md:py-32 bg-white">
		<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="text-center mb-12">
				<div class="text-xs font-[Cabin] font-bold tracking-[0.21em] text-[#d45c26] uppercase mb-4">
					The Vision
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-[Cabin] font-bold text-[#453c36] mb-6 leading-tight">
					What Success Looks Like
